**Relation between Moments and Cumulants**

The quantities cumulants of a _pdf_ can be expressed in terms of the moments by invoking _cumulant generating function, cgf_. The _cgf_, \(C_{X}\) for dummy variable t, is defined by

\[C_{X}(t) = lnM_{x}(t) \tag{4.18}\] \[\equiv c_{1}t+c_{2}\frac{t^{2}}{2!}+c_{3}\frac{t^{3}}{3!}+\cdot\cdot\cdot.\]

Here like _mgf_, there is no constant term in _cgf_ and \(c_{n}\) represents the nth order cumulants. The relation between cumulants and moments can be constructed as follows

\[\frac{dC_{X}(t)}{dt} = \frac{1}{M_{X}}\frac{dM_{x}(t)}{dt}\] \[\Rightarrow M_{X}(t)\frac{dC_{X}(t)}{dt} = \frac{dM_{x}(t)}{dt}\] \[\Rightarrow(1+\mu_{1}t+\mu_{2}\frac{t^{2}}{2!}+\mu_{3}\frac{t^{ 3}}{3!}+\cdot\cdot\cdot)\] \[\times\left(c_{1}+c_{2}t+c_{3}\frac{t^{2}}{2!}+\cdot\cdot\cdot\right)\] \[= (\mu_{1}+\mu_{2}t+\mu_{3}\frac{t^{2}}{2!}+\cdot\cdot).\]Now, comparing coefficients of like powers of \(t\) from both sides, it can be found

\[\mu_{1} = c_{1},\] \[\mu_{2} = c_{2}+c_{1}\mu_{1},\] \[\mu_{3} = c_{3}+2c_{2}\mu_{1}+c_{1}\mu_{2},\] \[\mu_{4} = c_{4}+3c_{3}\mu_{1}+3c_{2}\mu_{2}+c_{1}\mu_{3},\] \[\cdots\] \[\mu_{k} = c_{k}+{k-1\choose 1}c_{k-1}\mu_{1}+\cdots+{k-1\choose r}c_{k-r} \mu_{r}+c_{1}\mu_{k-1}. \tag{4.21}\]

Using above expressions, cumulants can also be expressed only in terms of moments as follows

\[c_{1} = \mu_{1},\] \[c_{2} = \mu_{2}-\mu_{1}^{2}=\nu_{2},\] \[c_{3} = \mu_{3}-3\mu_{2}\mu_{1}+2\mu_{1}^{3}=\nu_{3},\] \[c_{4} = \mu_{4}-4\mu_{3}\mu_{1}+12\mu_{2}\mu_{1}^{2}-3\mu_{2}^{2}-6\mu_{1 }^{4}=\nu_{4}-3\nu_{2}^{2}.\]

So, it can be easily understood that \(\mbox{mean}(M)\), standard deviation(\(\sigma\)), skewness(\(S\)) and kurtosis(\(\kappa\)) are related with cumulant as follows

\[\boxed{M=c_{1},\ \ \ \ \ \sigma=c_{2}^{1/2},\ \ \ \ \ S={c_{3}\over c_{2}^{3/2} },\ \ \ \ \ \kappa={c_{4}\over c_{2}^{2}}} \tag{4.23}\]

These observables are mainly used in this thesis work.

### Properties of the cumulants

1. Additivity:- if \(X_{1},X_{2},.....,X(n)\) are independent random variable, and \(C_{X_{i}}(t)\) for \(i\)=1, 2, 3,...,n is the _cgf_ for \(X_{i}\) the _cgf_ of \(S_{n}\) = \(c_{1}X_{1}+c_{2}X_{2}+.....+c_{n}X_{n}\) is given by \[C_{S_{n}}(t)=\sum_{i}C_{X_{i}}(c_{i}t).\] (4.24)
2. Shifting of origin:- For \(X\to X+a\), \(c_{1}\to c_{1}+a\). Where all other higher cumulant remains invariant.
3. Changing scale:- For \(X\to aX\), \(c_{r}\to a^{r}c_{r}\)

