Thermodynamical quantity, like pressure, entropy, etc., can be derived from the partition function, \(Z\) of the system and pressure, \(P\), can be expressed, in the thermodynamic limit, as follows

\[P(T,\mu_{B},\mu_{Q},\mu_{S})=\lim_{V\rightarrow\infty}\frac{T}{V}\ lnZ(T,V,\mu_ {B},\mu_{Q},\mu_{S}) \tag{4.25}\]

Here T, V are the temperature and volume of the system. The baryonic, charge and strangeness chemical potential can be represented by \(\mu_{B},\mu_{Q}\) and \(\mu_{S}\) respectively. The n\({}^{th}\) derivatives of thermodynamic susceptibilities of conserved quantities \(q\), can

